Gas Limit Identified.

UNBOUNDED WHILE LOOP CAUSING GAS LIMIT VULNERABILITY / DENIAL-OF-SERVICE
'''
The `addPayout` function contains an unbounded `while` loop:

```solidity
while (balance > participants[payoutOrder].payout) {
    uint payoutToSend = participants[payoutOrder].payout;
    participants[payoutOrder].etherAddress.send(payoutToSend);
    balance -= participants[payoutOrder].payout;
    payoutOrder += 1;
}
```

This loop is executed as part of the contract’s fallback function, meaning any user sending Ether to the contract can trigger it. Because there is no maximum iteration count or gas limit check, the loop may iterate through a very large number of pending participants in a single transaction. Each iteration updates storage, performs arithmetic, and makes an external `.send()` call, consuming a significant amount of gas. If enough pending payouts accumulate, the loop can exhaust the block gas limit, causing the entire transaction to fail.

This also creates a denial-of-service (DoS) risk: an attacker can intentionally contribute many small amounts to inflate the `participants` array and force subsequent legitimate deposits to hit the gas limit, making the contract unusable.

**Recommended mitigation/fix:**  
Refactor the payout mechanism to use a **pull payment / withdrawal pattern**. Instead of automatically iterating over all pending participants in a single transaction, store each participant’s claimable payout and let each participant call a dedicated `withdraw()` function to retrieve their own funds. If a loop is unavoidable, cap the number of iterations per transaction and process the remaining payouts over multiple transactions. Additionally, avoid using a loop for external calls; prefer individual claim functions to reduce gas risk and improve contract availability.
